"\n\n<h3>I. Normalcy as Closure</h3>\n\n<p>From Harding's inauguration in 1921 to Hoover's departure in 1933, three successive Republican administrations presented themselves as restoring constitutional normalcy after wartime mobilization, progressive reform, and Wilsonian internationalism. Harding's 1920 acceptance speech was explicit: wartime emergency had swept away every safeguard; \"representative popular government\" needed restoration. The program of normalcy combined withdrawal of federal economic regulation, fiscal consolidation, skepticism of organized group demands, and deliberate distance from supranational commitments.</p>\n\n<p>The central thesis of this essay is that normalcy did not close the remainder it inherited. Withdrawing the regulatory hand did not cure agricultural depression, reduce distributional inequality, or stabilize speculative finance. It postponed dealing with these pressures while they accumulated beneath a prosperous surface. Normalcy introduced the most passive and the most seductive form of the closure impulse: forgetting. Rather than suppressing the remainder or devising legal instruments to suspend it, forgetting simply removes it from the agenda while doing nothing about the underlying pressure. A remainder does not disappear because you stop looking at it.</p>\n\n<h3>II. Harding and the First Phase</h3>\n\n<p>Harding appointed figures of genuine distinction — Hughes at State, Mellon at Treasury, Hoover at Commerce — while surrounding himself with the Ohio Gang, a network of cronies whose venality later defined his legacy. The central scandal was Teapot Dome: Interior Secretary Fall secretly leased the Wyoming naval oil reserve to Harry Sinclair without competitive bidding, receiving suspicious loans in return. Fall became the first former Cabinet member convicted of a felony and imprisoned. The Teapot Dome proceedings also helped establish Congress's power to compel testimony (McGrain v. Daugherty, 1927).</p>\n\n<p>The deeper structural irony is instructive. The normalcy program aimed at restoring orderly, constitutional government after the progressive era's administrative expansion. What it partially restored instead was something closer to the patronage culture of the Gilded Age. When a government deliberately shrinks the professional administrative apparatus it does not necessarily recover a purer constitutional order; it may recover older, less formalized, and more corruptible arrangements. Harding died in office in August 1923 while traveling on his western tour, leaving an already scandal-ridden landscape for his successor to navigate.</p>\n\n<h3>III. Coolidge, Business Government, and Uneven Prosperity</h3>\n\n<p>Coolidge's governing philosophy was expressed in the speech of January 1925: \"the chief business of the American people is business.\" Under Mellon, successive tax acts reduced the top marginal rate from 73 percent in the wartime period to 25 percent by 1925. Coolidge favored balanced budgets, debt reduction, and low taxes; he reportedly joked that one of his greatest achievements was minding his own business.</p>\n\n<p>The prosperity of the 1920s was real. Automobiles, highways, radio, household electrification, aviation, and cinema created genuine economic growth and mass culture. But the distribution of those gains constituted structural warnings. Agriculture never participated in the decade's boom: commodity prices collapsed after 1920, and the farm sector experienced a rolling depression throughout the decade, with foreclosures and failures that long preceded the national collapse. Coolidge vetoed the McNary-Haugen farm relief bill twice, in 1927 and 1928, blocking the federal mechanism that might have begun addressing structural overproduction and low farm prices.</p>\n\n<p>Labor and distributional evidence points in the same direction. Union membership declined; real wages rose but lagged productivity growth. In sampled manufacturing, physical output in 1929 stood at 110 relative to 1923's 100, while workers' purchasing power stood at 94.4. Other research shows the income share of the top one or five percent in the late 1920s exceeding what it would be in 1939. The prosperity was broad enough to generate real political force, but not balanced enough to be structurally stable. Coolidge's agricultural veto is the clearest example: Congress, as the institution closer to constituents, had already sensed the pain and twice tried to respond. The president used the veto to push the visible remainder back off the agenda — and it accumulated further, invisible, until it could not be ignored.</p>\n\n<h3>IV. Exclusion, Nativism, and Enforced Order</h3>\n\n<p>The normalcy these administrations recovered was also a more exclusionary normalcy. The Immigration Act of 1924 (Johnson-Reed) imposed a national-origins quota system calibrated to the 1890 census, explicitly designed to favor Northern and Western European immigrants while sharply reducing arrivals from Southern and Eastern Europe. It effectively barred Asian immigration entirely by linking admissibility to eligibility for citizenship. This was not routine immigration restriction; it was a racially and ethnically ranked admissions system written into law.</p>\n\n<p>The Klan's second national rise belongs to the same evidentiary cluster. This iteration was a genuinely national movement — estimated membership ranging between three and eight million by various sources — targeting not only Black Americans but Catholics, Jews, immigrants, and anyone deemed outside \"100-percent Americanism.\" Prohibition, operating under the Eighteenth Amendment, achieved the reverse of its intended social ordering: it handed organized criminal networks a vast new revenue source and accelerated their nationalization.</p>\n\n<p>The structural point is revealing. In the same decade, these administrations relaxed regulatory oversight of economic power while tightening statutory control over who belonged. The two postures — hands-off toward markets, hands-on toward membership — shared a common vision: a market-operating, homogeneous, Northern-European-descended society. Withdrawing regulation and sharpening ethnic exclusion were, within that logic, two aspects of the same project of restoration. The searchlight that retreated on the economic axis cast a longer shadow on the racial and membership axis.</p>\n\n<h3>V. From Prosperity to Collapse</h3>\n\n<p>The 1928 election was the apex of the Republican decade's self-confidence. Hoover's campaign boasted that America had come nearer to eliminating poverty than any other society. These claims were not irrational in late 1928; they were the decade's governing faith stated with maximum confidence at precisely the wrong moment.</p>\n\n<p>The stock market crash began with Black Monday, October 28, 1929, when the Dow fell roughly 13 percent. But the depression was not primarily a stock-market event. It deepened through successive banking panics from 1930 to 1933, through the Federal Reserve's failure to prevent monetary contraction, and through the gold standard's deflationary international transmission. Annual unemployment rates tell the structural story: 3.2 percent in 1929, 8.7 in 1930, 15.9 in 1931, 23.6 in 1932, 24.9 in 1933. More than nine thousand banks failed in the four years before federal deposit insurance existed; depositor losses in 1931 alone exceeded total losses in the entire 1921-1929 period.</p>\n\n<p>The causal explanations can be usefully arrayed rather than collapsed into one. The monetarist account emphasizes the Fed's tightening in 1928-29 and failure to prevent monetary contraction. The Keynesian account stresses demand collapse after the market crash. Credit-channel research adds that financial failure had real nonmonetary effects that magnified the contraction beyond what money quantities alone can explain. International historians center the gold standard's role in transmitting deflationary pressure across borders. These schools are cumulative rather than mutually exclusive. From the perspective of this series, the Great Depression was the catastrophic return of the postponed economic remainder — agricultural depression, distributional imbalance, and speculative finance had been accumulating beneath the prosperous surface for a decade and returned in a form that could no longer be moved off any agenda.</p>\n\n<h3>VI. The Revised Hoover</h3>\n\n<p>Modern scholarship has substantially revised the caricature of Hoover as simply passive. He was not. He pressed business leaders to maintain wages, expanded public works, and created the Reconstruction Finance Corporation in January 1932 to provide emergency loans to banks, railroads, and later other institutions. The Emergency Relief and Construction Act of July 1932 authorized RFC loans to states for relief and public works. These were genuinely unprecedented departures from pre-Depression federal practice.</p>\n\n<p>The revision has limits, however. What constrained Hoover was not indifference but a specific set of constitutional and ideological commitments: to voluntarism, local responsibility, and limited federal action. His 1931 statement was unambiguous: relief must come through charity, mutual aid, and local government — not federal appropriations for charitable purposes. Direct federal income support to individuals on the scale the crisis demanded was the one thing his framework could not authorize without becoming a different framework entirely.</p>\n\n<p>Hoover's tragedy was therefore structural rather than personal. He stretched normalcy's assumptions as far as they could reach — credit support, coordination, public works, mortgage relief. What the crisis required was reconceiving the federal government as the society's primary economic guarantor. That reconception was precisely what his assumptions forbade. His failure was the failure of an entire framework of constitutional imagination: a framework that could improvise credit tools and public works programs but could not, on principled grounds, define the federal government as the backstop against mass destitution.</p>\n\n<h3>VII. The Bonus Army</h3>\n\n<p>The Bonus Expeditionary Force — World War I veterans demanding immediate payment of their service certificates — crystallized Hoover's failure in an image that could not be argued away. At its peak the encampment held more than eleven thousand veterans and family members. After Congress refused immediate payment and adjourned, Hoover ordered General MacArthur to clear the camp. MacArthur advanced with troops, tanks, and tear gas and burned the encampments — going well beyond the limited, minimally-forceful operation Hoover had instructed. The political consequences were severe: many Americans attributed the treatment of veterans directly to Hoover and his allies, and the electoral results were grave.</p>\n\n<p>The episode is the decade's moral and political summary. When the postponed economic remainder gathered at the nation's center in the form of hungry, desperate veterans, the normalcy order's answer was not relief but force. The camp was racially integrated — a detail of pointed irony in a decade defined by immigration restriction and Klan revival. Men who could not hold the color line in their shared desperation were met with their own government's tanks. A system that loosens all constraints on economic power while deploying full force against the victims of economic catastrophe has announced, in the clearest possible terms, the distribution of its loyalties.</p>\n\n<h3>VIII. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>The twelve years of Harding, Coolidge, and Hoover are best summarized by the essay's central thesis: normalcy mistook postponement for resolution.</p>\n\n<p>The 1920s were a real decade — of genuine growth, genuine technological transformation, genuine administrative retraction after wartime expansion, and a genuine popular desire for stability. They were simultaneously a decade in which agricultural depression, distributional imbalance, exclusionary immigration law, and speculative finance accumulated beneath the language of normalcy. The program assumed that withdrawing progressive intervention would leave behind a stable, limited-government baseline. It could not. The economic remainder does not disappear when you stop looking at it; it continues to accumulate where you are not looking.</p>\n\n<p>Hoover's deepest constraint was not his personality but the conceptual framework he inherited and embodied — one that could extend credit, coordinate business, and build infrastructure, but could not reconceive the federal government as the society's economic backstop. That reconception required the next act: a president in a wheelchair, a new political vocabulary, and a degree of institutional self-transformation unprecedented in peacetime American history. The remainder that normalcy had tried to close by forgetting was about to demand a response on a scale that forgetting had made far larger than it otherwise needed to be.</p>\n\n      "
